  "response_text": "We used the debugger in this lab because it allows us to observe program variables and hardware registers in real time. In embedded systems, variables are values stored in RAM and managed by the program, while hardware registers are special memory-mapped locations used to control and monitor the behavior of hardware components, like timers, GPIO, or communication peripherals. A tool like printf can only display values that the software can access and is often limited to program variables. It usually cannot access or display hardware registers directly, especially if they require specific timing or access patterns. Additionally, printf only shows values after the fact and introduces delays that can interfere with timing-sensitive operations. In contrast, a debugger lets us pause the program at any point using breakpoints and inspect the current state of both variables and hardware registers. This provides much deeper insight into how the system is behaving, especially when we\u2019re trying to understand low-level interactions between software and hardware.\\n",
